<body>
<main data-frwd-document="">
<article data-frwd-id="3c7a91d4-2b60-4e15-9a83-000000000001">

<header data-frwd-id="3c7a91d4-2b60-4e15-9a83-000000000002" class="masthead">
<p data-frwd-id="3c7a91d4-2b60-4e15-9a83-000000000003" class="eyebrow">Field service · HX-40</p>
<h1 data-frwd-id="3c7a91d4-2b60-4e15-9a83-000000000004">Calibrating a flow meter that has drifted</h1>
<p data-frwd-id="3c7a91d4-2b60-4e15-9a83-000000000005" class="standfirst">Twenty minutes, one reference cell, and a quiet room. This procedure assumes the meter is installed and the line is isolated.</p>
<p data-frwd-id="3c7a91d4-2b60-4e15-9a83-000000000006" class="byline"><span>Revision 4</span> <span>Applies to firmware 2.6 and later</span> <span>Northwind Instruments</span></p>
</header>

<div data-frwd-id="3c7a91d4-2b60-4e15-9a83-000000000007" class="body">

<section data-frwd-id="3c7a91d4-2b60-4e15-9a83-000000000008">
<h2 data-frwd-id="3c7a91d4-2b60-4e15-9a83-000000000009">Before you start</h2>
<p data-frwd-id="3c7a91d4-2b60-4e15-9a83-000000000010">A drifted meter reads plausibly. That is what makes it dangerous: nothing alarms, and the error accumulates quietly in whatever the readings feed. If a meter has been in service for more than eighteen months and nobody can name the last calibration date, treat it as drifted.</p>

<frwd-callout data-frwd-id="3c7a91d4-2b60-4e15-9a83-000000000011">
<aside data-frwd-id="3c7a91d4-2b60-4e15-9a83-000000000012" class="safety">
<h3 data-frwd-id="3c7a91d4-2b60-4e15-9a83-000000000013">Isolate before you open</h3>
<p data-frwd-id="3c7a91d4-2b60-4e15-9a83-000000000014">The sensing element stays energised for up to ninety seconds after the supply is cut. Wait for the status ring to go dark before removing the cover — not for the display to blank, which happens sooner.</p>
</aside>
</frwd-callout>
</section>

<section data-frwd-id="3c7a91d4-2b60-4e15-9a83-000000000015">
<h2 data-frwd-id="3c7a91d4-2b60-4e15-9a83-000000000016">Procedure</h2>

<ol data-frwd-id="3c7a91d4-2b60-4e15-9a83-000000000017" class="steps">

<li data-frwd-id="3c7a91d4-2b60-4e15-9a83-000000000018">
<h3 data-frwd-id="3c7a91d4-2b60-4e15-9a83-000000000019">Record the standing reading</h3>
<p data-frwd-id="3c7a91d4-2b60-4e15-9a83-000000000020">Before touching anything, note what the meter currently reports at zero flow. This is the number the calibration is measured against, and it is the only chance to capture it.</p>
</li>

<li data-frwd-id="3c7a91d4-2b60-4e15-9a83-000000000021">
<h3 data-frwd-id="3c7a91d4-2b60-4e15-9a83-000000000022">Fit the reference cell</h3>
<p data-frwd-id="3c7a91d4-2b60-4e15-9a83-000000000023">Seat the cell against the upstream flange and tighten opposing bolts in pairs. The cell reads high if it is skewed, and skew is difficult to see once the housing is closed.</p>
</li>

<li data-frwd-id="3c7a91d4-2b60-4e15-9a83-000000000024">
<h3 data-frwd-id="3c7a91d4-2b60-4e15-9a83-000000000025">Run the settling cycle</h3>
<p data-frwd-id="3c7a91d4-2b60-4e15-9a83-000000000026">Hold the mode button until the ring pulses, then release. The cycle takes about four seconds and must not be interrupted; a partial cycle leaves the previous calibration in place and reports success anyway.</p>

<frwd-video data-frwd-id="3c7a91d4-2b60-4e15-9a83-000000000027">
<figure data-frwd-id="3c7a91d4-2b60-4e15-9a83-000000000028">
<video controls="" height="180" playsinline="" poster="data:image/webp;base64," preload="metadata" width="320">
<source src="data:video/webm;base64," type="video/webm">
<source src="data:video/mp4;base64," type="video/mp4">
</video>
<div data-frwd-id="3c7a91d4-2b60-4e15-9a83-000000000029" class="print-substitute">
<img alt="The status ring pulsing green through the four-second settling cycle." height="180" src="data:image/webp;base64," width="320">
</div>
<figcaption data-frwd-id="3c7a91d4-2b60-4e15-9a83-000000000030"><b>The settling cycle, in real time.</b> The ring pulses green for four seconds and then holds steady. If it flickers amber at any point, the cycle did not complete. On paper this figure appears as the poster frame above.</figcaption>
</figure>
</frwd-video>
</li>

<li data-frwd-id="3c7a91d4-2b60-4e15-9a83-000000000031">
<h3 data-frwd-id="3c7a91d4-2b60-4e15-9a83-000000000032">Match the reference tone</h3>
<p data-frwd-id="3c7a91d4-2b60-4e15-9a83-000000000033">With the cover off, the meter emits a tone proportional to the sensed rate. A correctly calibrated HX-40 at the reference rate sounds like this:</p>

<frwd-audio data-frwd-id="3c7a91d4-2b60-4e15-9a83-000000000034">
<figure data-frwd-id="3c7a91d4-2b60-4e15-9a83-000000000035">
<audio controls="" preload="metadata">
<source src="data:audio/ogg;base64," type="audio/ogg">
<source src="data:audio/mpeg;base64," type="audio/mpeg">
</audio>
<p data-frwd-id="3c7a91d4-2b60-4e15-9a83-000000000036" class="print-substitute">Reference tone: 520 Hz, held for four seconds. A meter reading high sounds sharp against it; one reading low sounds flat and unsteady.</p>
<figcaption data-frwd-id="3c7a91d4-2b60-4e15-9a83-000000000037"><b>Reference tone, 520 Hz.</b> Beating against the meter's own tone means the two rates differ. The description above is what appears in print.</figcaption>
</figure>
</frwd-audio>
</li>

<li data-frwd-id="3c7a91d4-2b60-4e15-9a83-000000000038">
<h3 data-frwd-id="3c7a91d4-2b60-4e15-9a83-000000000039">Confirm the seal</h3>
<p data-frwd-id="3c7a91d4-2b60-4e15-9a83-000000000040">Close the housing and check the gasket seat at three points. The seat is correct when the witness mark is fully covered.</p>

<frwd-gallery data-frwd-id="3c7a91d4-2b60-4e15-9a83-000000000041">
<figure data-frwd-id="3c7a91d4-2b60-4e15-9a83-000000000042">
<div data-frwd-id="3c7a91d4-2b60-4e15-9a83-000000000043" class="gallery">
<img alt="Correct gasket seat: the witness mark is fully covered." height="150" src="data:image/webp;base64,UklGRowAAABXRUJQVlA4IIAAAABwCwCdASrwAJYAPpFIoUylpCMiIEgAsBIJaW7hd1AAHogFiA22i4QZBDVUmu20XCDIIaqk122i4QZBDVUmu20XCDIIaqk122i4QZBDVUmu20XCDIIaqk122i4QZBDVUmu20W+gAP7ryJH/7oe+ryqMxvUHQhk8CZwgAAAAAAAAAA==" width="240">
<img alt="Under-tightened: the witness mark is partly visible along the lower edge." height="150" src="data:image/webp;base64,UklGRowAAABXRUJQVlA4IIAAAAAQCwCdASrwAJYAPpFIoU0lpCMiIEgAsBIJaW7hdqAAJ7Xoq4QZBDVUmu20XCDIIaqk122i4QZBDVUmu20XCDIIaqk122i4QZBDVUmu20XCDIIaqk122i4QZBDVUmu20W+gAP7/cbN/gy22h99yfq00zZ0EpZyzQ+8IAAAAAAAAAA==" width="240">
<img alt="Over-tightened: the gasket has extruded past the seat." height="150" src="data:image/webp;base64,UklGRo4AAABXRUJQVlA4IIIAAAAQCwCdASrwAJYAPpFIoU0lpCMiIEgAsBIJaW7hdqAAJ7Xoq4QZBDVUmu20XCDIIaqk122i4QZBDVUmu20XCDIIaqk122i4QZBDVUmu20XCDIIaqk122i4QZBDVUmu20W+gAP7/WoX/4d3N161v87bRT+fbb/HQRGYRFWEAAAAAAAAA" width="240">
</div>
<figcaption data-frwd-id="3c7a91d4-2b60-4e15-9a83-000000000044"><b>Gasket seat, left to right:</b> correct, under-tightened, over-tightened. Under-tightening is the common failure and the one that passes a pressure test.</figcaption>
</figure>
</frwd-gallery>
</li>

</ol>
</section>

<section data-frwd-id="3c7a91d4-2b60-4e15-9a83-000000000045">
<h2 data-frwd-id="3c7a91d4-2b60-4e15-9a83-000000000046">If it will not settle</h2>
<p data-frwd-id="3c7a91d4-2b60-4e15-9a83-000000000047">A meter that refuses three consecutive settling cycles is usually reporting a sensor fault as a calibration fault.</p>

<frwd-disclosure data-frwd-id="3c7a91d4-2b60-4e15-9a83-000000000048" data-frwd-expanded="false">
<button aria-expanded="false" data-frwd-disclosure-toggle="" type="button">Fault codes and what they actually mean</button>
<div data-frwd-id="3c7a91d4-2b60-4e15-9a83-000000000049" data-frwd-disclosure-body="">
<p data-frwd-id="3c7a91d4-2b60-4e15-9a83-000000000050">Code 14 is documented as "calibration timeout" and is almost always a loose reference cell. Code 21 is documented as "sensor fault" and is usually condensation; leave the housing open for ten minutes and repeat.</p>
<p data-frwd-id="3c7a91d4-2b60-4e15-9a83-000000000051">Code 33 is the only one that means what it says. Replace the sensing element.</p>
</div>
</frwd-disclosure>
</section>

</div>
</article>
</main>

</body></html>